;; x86i's check's classes (bin/repr-asm x86i-check; doc/asm/AMD64.md §10, AX5-DESIGN §4): every row of
;; test/asm/x86i-results-amd64.edn that does not pass is in one of these. In goi's format. Read with the Clojure reader.
[
 {:id :unimplemented
  :title "an instruction no family claims"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "x86i decodes it (or refuses its encoding: VEX, EVEX, XOP, REX2, 3DNow!) and raises SIGILL, naming it on stderr, never a silent wrong result; the families arrive slice by slice (AX5.4: rcl/rcr, shld/shrd, bit, enter, signal frames; AX5.5: BMI, ADX, cmpxchg16b, rdrand/rdseed/rdpid, SSE/SSE2; still unclaimed: SSE3 and later, AVX, MMX, rcpps/rsqrtps (each vendor's own approximations), a rounding mode other than nearest, DAZ, FTZ and unmasked SIMD exceptions (AX8))"
  :decided "AX5-DESIGN §1, AXS54 (a)"}
 {:id :encoding-deferred
  :title "an encoding x86i's Go decoder does not read yet"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "EVEX, XOP, REX2, 3DNow! and VEX's maps 5-7 are refused by x86i's decoder (`!unsupported`; SIGILL at run time) until the families that need them (AX8's AVX-512, APX); the decode check (x86i-decode-check) counts them per file, every other instruction equal to arbace.asm.amd64.decode's"
  :decided "AX5-DESIGN §2, AXS69 (a), AXS81 (a)"}
 {:id :x87
  :title "x87 instructions"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "the x87 file and its instructions are AX6's"
  :decided "AX5-DESIGN §4"}
 {:id :cpuid
  :title "a program that branches on cpuid in a CPU-compared run"
  :kind :by-design
  :why "x86i answers cpuid with its own model (AXS59) and this CPU cannot fault cpuid, so the CPU's run (x86i/NAME) and the sys mode's lockstep (lockstep/NAME) of a program that writes or branches on the answer (`# cpu: cpuid` in its header) see another answer; its own stated verdict judges it, and the step mode (step/NAME), which overwrites the native's answer with the model's, must be equal. None since x86-print (2026-11-22): the native's cpuid is answered with the model in both, as the step mode answers it (AXS58 (a)'s rule): the sys mode puts an int3 at each cpuid x86i is about to run before the native runs to the same system call, and the CPU's run of such a program is `x86i cpu`, the program under ptrace with an int3 at each cpuid of its code (decode-text's sweep), x86i.CpuidModel answering both (controls inject-cpuid-model, lockstep-cpuid-model: x86i's own answer altered, X86I_INJECT=cpuid:model); kept for a program whose cpuid the patching misses"
  :decided "AX5-DESIGN §3, AXS58 (a); emptied by x86-print"}
 {:id :vendor
  :title "a flag or result the SDM leaves undefined and the APM defines"
  :kind :by-design
  :why "x86i does what the APM says; another vendor's CPU differs"
  :decided "AX5-DESIGN §1, AXS55 (a)"}
 {:id :threads
  :title "clone, fork, vfork, execve"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "one thread in AX5: x86i returns -ENOSYS"
  :decided "AX5-DESIGN §1"}
 {:id :nondeterministic
  :title "a program whose output depends on time, randomness or addresses"
  :kind :by-design
  :why "the CPU's run and x86i's differ by the world, not by the machine; the lockstep's world list (AXS58) copies it"
  :decided "AX5-DESIGN §3"}
]
